    <h2 id="why">0. Why this document exists</h2>
    <p>Availability work was spread across <strong>six overlapping documents</strong> totalling roughly 530&nbsp;KB, most of which describe <em>hardware that is not in service</em>. That fragmentation was itself a reliability risk: it was not possible to answer "what are we actually doing?" without reading four contradictory roadmaps. This document is now the <strong>single source of truth</strong>. &sect;10 records exactly what was absorbed and what was retired, so nothing of value was silently dropped.</p>

    <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-left: 4px solid var(--link-color); padding: var(--spacing-medium); margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <p style="margin:0;"><strong>The single biggest source of confusion  -  now settled:</strong> the December 2025 plan set was written against a <strong>3-host Proxmox cluster</strong> (<code>proxmoxws</code> .11, <code>proxmox720</code> .12, <code>proxmoxt210</code> .3) and repeatedly names <strong>192.168.1.198</strong> as "the production database". <strong>Both statements are false today.</strong> Production runs on bare metal, and the primary database is <strong>192.168.1.20:3307</strong>. Following those docs would have migrated the wrong database on the wrong machines.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="goal">1. Goal</h2>
    <p><strong>Deliver the site to all users 99% of the time</strong>, moving from a single fragile container to an upwardly scalable system, while making full use of hardware already owned.</p>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted-color); font-size: var(--font-size-small);">99% availability = up to ~7h20m of downtime per month. That budget is consumed by <em>unplanned</em> outages; planned rolling deploys should cost zero once Phase D lands.</p>

    <h2 id="current">2. Verified current state (probed 2026-08-07, not assumed)</h2>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Host</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Role today</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Verified</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">HA relevance</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>.126</strong> production1</td><td style="padding:6px;">Live Catalyst app (<code>comserv2-web-prod</code>), nginx :80, app :5000</td><td style="padding:6px;">up; healthy</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>SPOF  -  the whole site</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>.199</strong> workstation</td><td style="padding:6px;">Image build host; Ollama; dev workstation</td><td style="padding:6px;">up; Docker present</td><td style="padding:6px;">2nd app node + registry host</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>.20</strong> db-server-1</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>PRIMARY DB</strong>  -  MariaDB <code>ency</code>, port 3307, priority 1</td><td style="padding:6px;">port 3307 open</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>SPOF  -  hard outage if lost</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>.198</strong></td><td style="padding:6px;">Backup DB server (MySQL/MariaDB on 3306)</td><td style="padding:6px;">up; 3306 open; <em>currency unverified</em></td><td style="padding:6px;">Least-used box  -  DB replica candidate</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted-color); font-size: var(--font-size-small);"><strong>Standing constraint:</strong> <code>.20</code> is <strong>database-only</strong> by explicit decision  -  do not co-locate a registry, k3s node, or any other service on it. <strong>Unverified:</strong> .198 CPU/RAM/disk headroom and its Docker status could not be probed (different SSH credentials); Phase F.0 must establish these before committing it to a role.</p>

    <h3>2b. What the 2026-08-07 outage proved</h3>
    <p>A monitor-cron bug deleted the only production container and could not restore it; the site served an nginx default page for roughly 100 minutes. The script defects are fixed (commit <code>86e1dce6</code>, todo 1959), but the <strong>architectural</strong> lesson stands and is the whole justification for this plan: <strong>one container on one host means any single fault is a total outage.</strong> No amount of recovery-script hardening changes that.</p>

    <h2 id="target">3. Target architecture &mdash; and the endpoint</h2>
    <p>The endpoint is <strong>standard Kubernetes</strong>. k3s is not a different destination; it is <em>certified, conformant Kubernetes</em> in a small package. Everything authored on k3s &mdash; Deployments, Services, Ingress, Secrets, CronJobs &mdash; is standard K8s YAML that moves to a full distribution unchanged. That is precisely why k3s is the right first step rather than a detour: <strong>it lets us reach the endpoint incrementally instead of in one high-risk jump.</strong></p>

    <h3 id="propagation">3a. The core problem: one proven container, propagated automatically</h3>
    <p>This is the requirement that matters most day to day, and the one the old plan set never addressed. Today a container is built on .199 and then <strong>each host must be updated by hand</strong>. That is the source of drift, of "which box is running what?", and of the manual OPNsense flip.</p>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Concern</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Today (manual)</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Target (automatic)</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Getting a new image onto a node</td><td style="padding:6px;">Human pulls/updates on each host in turn</td><td style="padding:6px;">Nodes pull the pinned digest themselves from the registry</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Are all nodes on the same build?</td><td style="padding:6px;">Unknown without checking each one</td><td style="padding:6px;"><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> &mdash; one authoritative answer</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Rollout of a bad build</td><td style="padding:6px;">All-at-once; discovered by users</td><td style="padding:6px;">Rolling update, gated by readiness probes; stops on failure</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Undo</td><td style="padding:6px;">Manual retag and redeploy per host</td><td style="padding:6px;"><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> &mdash; one command, all nodes</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Adding a third node later</td><td style="padding:6px;">Repeat the whole manual setup</td><td style="padding:6px;">Join the node; the scheduler places pods on it automatically</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Promotion model:</strong> an image is built ONCE on .199 and promoted by <strong>immutable digest</strong> (never a floating <code>:latest</code>) through dev &rarr; staging &rarr; production. "Proven" must mean <em>this exact digest passed its gate</em>. Retagging <code>:latest</code> is what makes "the container that worked" unprovable, and it is retired by this plan.</p>

    <h3>3b. Component target</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>k3s cluster, 2 nodes:</strong> .126 (server + worker) and .199 (agent + worker). Conformant K8s API from day one.</li>
        <li><strong>Image registry on .199</strong> (<code>registry:2</code>, port 5000, insecure/HTTP on LAN) &mdash; the single source every node pulls from. This is what makes automatic propagation possible at all.</li>
        <li><strong>Deployment with replicas=2</strong> behind a health-checked Service/Ingress. A node or pod dying removes it from rotation automatically &mdash; <strong>no manual OPNsense flip</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Load balancing:</strong> ingress spreads traffic across both pods, so the second node <em>earns its keep</em> continuously rather than sitting dormant &mdash; the "maximum hardware use" requirement.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared state must be external to the pod:</strong> <code>comserv2_sessions</code>, <code>comserv2_nfs_data</code>, redis &mdash; see &sect;6.</li>
        <li><strong>DB HA (Phase F):</strong> .198 promoted from stale backup to a real replica of .20, closing the last hard SPOF.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="hardware-use">3c. Using all the hardware, despite mixed capability</h3>
    <p>The hosts are not uniform and several are far from state of the art. Kubernetes is well suited to exactly this: nodes are labelled by capability and workloads are placed accordingly, so weaker machines still contribute rather than being excluded.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Node labels + <code>nodeSelector</code>/affinity:</strong> heavy work (image builds, Whisper, OpenSCAD) pinned to capable nodes; the web app, which is light, runs anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Resource requests/limits:</strong> a small node honestly declares its capacity and simply receives fewer pods &mdash; it is used, not overwhelmed.</li>
        <li><strong>Taints/tolerations:</strong> keep the DB host and any latency-sensitive box free of general workload (.20 stays database-only).</li>
        <li><strong>Anti-affinity:</strong> force the two web replicas onto <em>different</em> nodes &mdash; without it, Kubernetes may schedule both on one host and the HA is illusory.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="roles">3d. Role assignment must be EVALUATED, never assumed</h2>
    <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--caution-color); padding: var(--spacing-small); margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <strong>Retracted assumption.</strong> An earlier draft assumed .199 (workstation) should host the registry <em>because it is the most powerful machine</em>. That reasoning is invalid: raw capability is not availability. A machine that runs AI models can be heavily loaded exactly when it is needed, and may be effectively unavailable for Docker work.
    </div>
    <p><strong>Measured 2026-08-07 (not assumed):</strong></p>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Host</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">CPU</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Load avg</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">RAM free</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Disk</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Reading</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">.199 workstation</td><td style="padding:6px;">8 core + Quadro M4000</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>8.10</strong> (= 100% of 8 cores)</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>0 GB free</strong> / 31 GB</td><td style="padding:6px;">86% (66 GB free)</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>Saturated.</strong> 8B model resident in Ollama; sustained load 8.1&ndash;8.7 over 15 min</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">.126 production1</td><td style="padding:6px;">2 core</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>0.06</strong> (idle)</td><td style="padding:6px;">24 GB avail / 26 GB</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>88% (3.5 GB free)</strong></td><td style="padding:6px;">Idle CPU but <strong>disk-critical</strong>; uptime 48 days</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>This <strong>confirms the concern and refutes the earlier draft</strong>: the "most powerful" machine is the <em>busiest</em> one, while the "production" machine is nearly idle but almost out of disk. Neither is an obvious registry host &mdash; .199 for contention, .126 for 3.5 GB of headroom. A registry needs disk and availability, not GPU.</p>
    <p><strong>Therefore every phase carries its own role-evaluation todo</strong> (the <code>.0</code> todo of each sub-project) which must answer, with measurements taken under realistic load:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Which host should hold this role, and <strong>on what evidence</strong> &mdash; not which is nominally strongest.</li>
        <li>What happens to this role when the host is <strong>under peak AI/build load</strong>, not when idle.</li>
        <li><strong>Where does this role go if that host is unavailable?</strong> Every role needs a named second home.</li>
        <li>Does this role conflict with another role already on that host?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="quorum">3e. How many nodes? The 2-node quorum problem</h2>
    <p>A direct answer to "if both production1 and workstation are down or overloaded, what is the next failover  -  so if we need 3+ nodes, is k3s where we should start?"</p>
    <p><strong>Two nodes is not a real HA cluster.</strong> It survives the loss of a <em>worker</em>, but not the loss of the <em>control plane</em>, and it cannot form a quorum: with 2 nodes there is no majority, so a network split leaves neither side safely authoritative. Today .126 would be the single k3s server &mdash; losing it loses cluster management even if .199 keeps serving.</p>
    <p><strong>But this does not argue against starting with k3s</strong>, because k3s itself supports 3-node embedded-etcd HA control planes. The node count is a <em>cluster topology</em> question, not a <em>distribution</em> question. Starting on k3s does not cap us at 2 nodes.</p>
    <p><strong>The honest gap:</strong> we do not currently have a credible third node. .20 is deliberately DB-only (user-confirmed constraint), and .198's capability is unverified. So the third-node question must be <em>answered by evaluation</em>, not assumed &mdash; see the candidate list in &sect;3f. This is why Phase B carries an explicit topology-decision todo rather than a hardcoded "2 nodes".</p>

    <h2 id="progression">3f. The progression ladder &mdash; beyond k3s</h2>
    <p>A previous draft showed only a k3s solution with no migration up the tree. Corrected: below is the full ladder, each rung with the trigger that justifies climbing to it. <strong>We stop at the lowest rung that meets the availability target</strong>; the ladder exists so the next step is always known and pre-justified.</p>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Rung</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Solution</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Survives</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Trigger to climb</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">0</td><td style="padding:6px;">Single container (today)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Nothing</td><td style="padding:6px;">Already failed &mdash; 2026-08-07 outage</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">1</td><td style="padding:6px;">2-node k3s, replicas=2</td><td style="padding:6px;">One worker/pod failure</td><td style="padding:6px;">Control-plane loss unacceptable</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">2</td><td style="padding:6px;">3-node k3s, embedded-etcd HA control plane</td><td style="padding:6px;">Any one node, incl. control plane</td><td style="padding:6px;">Whole-site/LAN loss unacceptable</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">3</td><td style="padding:6px;">Geographic node over <strong>ZeroTier</strong> (offsite/remote hardware)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Loss of the physical site</td><td style="padding:6px;">Need capacity/SLA beyond owned hardware</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">4</td><td style="padding:6px;">External/cloud node or managed K8s in the same cluster</td><td style="padding:6px;">Site + hardware limits</td><td style="padding:6px;">Scale/compliance/ops beyond self-hosting</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Rungs 1&ndash;2 are the same technology</strong> &mdash; adding a server node, not a migration. Rungs 3&ndash;4 add nodes to the <em>same</em> cluster, so they are also not rewrites. This is the "progressively better solutions" path: one architecture, climbed incrementally.</p>

    <h3 id="external">3g. External and ZeroTier options (previously missing)</h3>
    <p>The plan wrongly considered only LAN hardware. Real options that must be <strong>evaluated, not assumed</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>ZeroTier-connected offsite node.</strong> Already proven in this estate: ZeroTier network 172.30.0.0/16 is live, and <code>zerotier_ency.json</code> defines a working priority-3 DB path at 172.30.161.222. A remote machine can therefore join as a k3s node over ZeroTier &mdash; the only option that survives losing the physical site, which no amount of LAN hardware achieves. Caveat to measure: k3s tolerates WAN latency for workers far better than for etcd peers, so a ZeroTier node is a strong <em>worker</em> candidate and a poor <em>control-plane</em> candidate.</li>
        <li><strong>Unidentified LAN hosts.</strong> The IP registry lists .144, .175, .200, .201, .205, .220 as Active/Unknown. Some may be viable nodes. <strong>Identify before buying or renting anything.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Proxmox capacity.</strong> Hypervisors at .2/.3/.12 exist; VMs on them could serve as nodes. Prior plans assumed a Proxmox cluster was the target and were superseded for being unreal &mdash; but the hardware is listed as Active and should be <em>verified</em>, not dismissed by reflex.</li>
        <li><strong>Small cloud instance</strong> as a third control-plane member purely for quorum &mdash; cheap, and solves the 2-node split-brain problem without new physical hardware.</li>
        <li><strong>Do nothing beyond rung 2</strong> &mdash; a legitimate outcome if measured availability meets the target.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--spacing-small);"><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">&uarr; Back to Top</a></p>

    <h2 id="pushing">4. Does k3s eliminate "pushing to production"?  -  No.</h2>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Phase</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Current</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">With k3s</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Build</td><td style="padding:6px;">.199</td><td style="padding:6px;">.199 (unchanged)</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Push target</td><td style="padding:6px;">Docker Hub / LAN registry</td><td style="padding:6px;">LAN registry on .199</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Deploy to live</td><td style="padding:6px;">manual OPNsense &rarr; .199 container</td><td style="padding:6px;"><code>kubectl set image</code> &rarr; rolling update across nodes</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Failover</td><td style="padding:6px;">human flips OPNsense</td><td style="padding:6px;">automatic (health check + reschedule)</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Bad deploy</td><td style="padding:6px;">manual rollback / image retag</td><td style="padding:6px;"><code>kubectl rollout undo</code></td></tr>
    </table>

    <h2 id="roadmap">5. Phased roadmap</h2>
    <p>Each phase is a todo in project <a href="[% c.uri_for('/project/details') %]?project_id=202" style="color: var(--link-color);">#202 (INFRA-HA)</a> and carries a GATE that must be demonstrated before the next phase starts.</p>
    <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--caution-color); padding: var(--spacing-small); margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <strong>Structural correction: each phase is a SUB-PROJECT, not a todo.</strong> An earlier draft mapped each phase to a single todo (1905&ndash;1910). That is wrong &mdash; "install k3s" is not one doable unit of work. Each phase becomes a <strong>sub-project of #202</strong> (the schema supports this: <code>Project.parent_id</code> with a <code>sub_projects</code> relation, and the UI accepts <code>?parent_id=</code>), broken into small todos that can each be finished in one sitting.
        <p style="margin: var(--spacing-small) 0 0 0;">The existing todos 1905&ndash;1910 are <strong>converted to sub-projects</strong>, not deleted &mdash; their history and the phase gates are preserved.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Every sub-project starts with the same two mandatory todos</strong>, which the earlier draft omitted entirely:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>.0 ROLE EVALUATION</strong> &mdash; which host should hold this role, on measured evidence under realistic load, and <strong>where the role fails over to</strong> (&sect;3d). No phase may proceed on an assumed host.</li>
        <li><strong>.1 DEPENDENCY CHECK</strong> &mdash; does this phase need an auth/credential change? If so, raise the todo <em>on the authentication plan</em> (&sect;11) and accept being blocked.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every phase also states <strong>WHY NEXT</strong> &mdash; the specific limitation that remains after this phase, which is the sole justification for taking the following step. If a phase's WHY NEXT does not actually hurt us, <strong>stop there</strong>: this roadmap is a sequence of earned decisions, not a march to Kubernetes for its own sake.</p>
    <div style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); padding: var(--spacing-small); margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <strong>The chain in one line each:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>A Registry</strong> &mdash; without one shared image source, no node can pull a build automatically.</li>
            <li><strong>B k3s</strong> &mdash; a registry alone still needs a human to run the image on each host; we need a scheduler.</li>
            <li><strong>C Deployment</strong> &mdash; a scheduler with no declared desired state cannot self-heal or roll out.</li>
            <li><strong>D Cutover</strong> &mdash; a Deployment nobody routes traffic to protects no users.</li>
            <li><strong>E Failover proof</strong> &mdash; untested HA is a claim, not availability.</li>
            <li><strong>F DB HA</strong> &mdash; app HA still dies with the single database; 99% is unreachable until this closes.</li>
            <li><strong>G Automated propagation</strong> &mdash; rollout is reliable but still human-triggered per release.</li>
            <li><strong>H Full Kubernetes</strong> &mdash; only if scale/features outgrow k3s; otherwise deliberately not taken.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Phase A - Registry on .199</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> a test tag pushed from .199 pulls successfully on .126.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Stand up <code>registry:2</code> on .199, port 5000, with a persistent volume.</li>
        <li>Add <code>192.168.1.199:5000</code> to <code>insecure-registries</code> on BOTH .126 and .199.</li>
        <li>Naming follows the system, not the feature: <code>comserv2_registry_data</code> for the volume.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project A todos:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A.0 ROLE EVAL:</strong> which host hosts the registry? <strong>Do not default to .199.</strong> Measured 2026-08-07: .199 load 8.10/8 cores, 0 GB RAM free (AI models); .126 idle but only 3.5 GB disk free. Registry needs disk + availability, not GPU. Evaluate both, plus unidentified hosts (&sect;3g). Record evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>A.1 DEPENDENCY:</strong> does the registry need auth (push credentials)? If yes &rarr; todo on auth plan.</li>
        <li>A.2 Free disk on the chosen host if needed (.126 at 88% cannot host a registry as-is).</li>
        <li>A.3 Stand up <code>registry:2</code> with persistent volume <code>comserv2_registry_data</code>.</li>
        <li>A.4 Configure <code>insecure-registries</code> on all nodes.</li>
        <li>A.5 Push/pull round-trip test; record the digest.</li>
        <li>A.6 Decide registry retention/pruning policy (an unpruned registry will fill the disk and recreate the outage).</li>
        <li><strong>A.7 FAILOVER:</strong> what happens to deploys when the registry host is down or saturated? Document the answer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> the registry makes one proven image <em>available</em> to every node, but nothing yet <em>places</em> it. A human still runs it host by host. That gap is what Phase B closes.</p>

    <h3>Phase B - k3s install</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> <code>kubectl get nodes</code> shows both nodes Ready, and the existing compose container on .126 is still serving untouched.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Install k3s server on .126; join .199 as an agent.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify-before-execute:</strong> k3s must not disturb the live compose container until Phase D proves the Deployment.</li>
        <li>Confirm k3s does not bind port 5000 (the app) or collide with the registry port on .199.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project B todos:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>B.0 ROLE EVAL:</strong> which host is the k3s <em>server</em> (control plane)? A saturated host is a poor control plane; .199's sustained load 8.1+ argues against it.</li>
        <li><strong>B.1 DEPENDENCY:</strong> cluster access credentials (kubeconfig) &rarr; auth plan if a managed mechanism is required.</li>
        <li><strong>B.2 TOPOLOGY DECISION (blocking):</strong> 2 nodes or 3? Per &sect;3e, 2 nodes cannot form quorum and leaves a single control plane. Identify a credible third node from &sect;3g candidates (unidentified LAN hosts, Proxmox VM, ZeroTier offsite, small cloud quorum member) or record explicitly that we accept rung 1 for now.</li>
        <li>B.3 Install k3s server on the chosen host.</li>
        <li>B.4 Join remaining node(s) as agents.</li>
        <li>B.5 Label nodes by capability; taint any that must not take general workload (&sect;3c).</li>
        <li>B.6 Verify no port collision with the live app or registry.</li>
        <li>B.7 Confirm the live compose container is still serving, untouched.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> we now have a scheduler, but we have not told it what we want running. An empty cluster self-heals nothing. Phase C declares the desired state.</p>

    <h3>Phase C - App Deployment manifest</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> both pods Running and independently serving <code>/health</code>; no credential present in any image layer.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Deployment (replicas=2, image <code>&lt;registry&gt;/comserv-web-prod:&lt;sha&gt;</code>), Service (ClusterIP), Ingress with a health probe on <code>/health</code>.</li>
        <li>Readiness probe must gate traffic; liveness probe must allow a <strong>60s+ start_period</strong>  -  the app genuinely needs ~90s to become healthy (measured 2026-08-07). Too short a probe recreates the exact kill-loop that caused the outage.</li>
        <li>DB credentials via k8s Secret sourced from <code>db_config.json</code>  -  never baked into the image (IMGSEC).</li>
        <li>Mount shared session/NFS/redis state (&sect;6).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project C todos:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>C.0 ROLE EVAL:</strong> which nodes should run web pods, and what are their resource requests/limits? Confirm anti-affinity so both replicas cannot land on one node.</li>
        <li><strong>C.1 DEPENDENCY (likely BLOCKING):</strong> AUTH-R1 &mdash; credentials must reach a pod without hand-placed files. Raise on the auth plan; this phase waits.</li>
        <li>C.2 Write Deployment manifest (replicas=2, image pinned by digest).</li>
        <li>C.3 Service + Ingress with <code>/health</code> probe.</li>
        <li>C.4 Tune probes: liveness <code>start_period</code> 60s+; app measured at ~90s to healthy. Too short recreates the outage kill-loop.</li>
        <li>C.5 Mount shared session/NFS/redis state (&sect;6).</li>
        <li>C.6 Verify no credential is present in any image layer (IMGSEC).</li>
        <li>C.7 Both pods serving <code>/health</code> independently.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> two healthy pods now exist and update by digest across both nodes automatically &mdash; but real users are still being served by the old single compose container, so none of that protects anyone yet. Phase D moves the traffic.</p>

    <h3>Phase D - Cutover (zero-downtime intent)</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> external traffic served by the ingress with no user-visible change; rollback path rehearsed.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Run the Deployment <em>alongside</em> the existing compose container; validate both serve identical responses.</li>
        <li>Switch the OPNsense / external entry to the k3s ingress.</li>
        <li>Retire the manual-switch compose standby only after a stable period.</li>
        <li>Keep the compose path documented as an emergency escape hatch until Phase E passes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project D todos:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>D.0 ROLE EVAL:</strong> which host terminates external traffic (ingress/OPNsense entry), and where does that role fail over to?</li>
        <li><strong>D.1 DEPENDENCY (BLOCKING):</strong> AUTH-R2 &mdash; sessions must be valid across both replicas or balanced users get logged out at random. Raise on the auth plan.</li>
        <li>D.2 Run Deployment alongside the compose container; compare responses.</li>
        <li>D.3 Rehearse rollback BEFORE cutover, not after.</li>
        <li>D.4 Switch the external entry to the ingress.</li>
        <li>D.5 Monitor a stable period; keep compose as escape hatch.</li>
        <li>D.6 Confirm pods are not falling back to a deprecated credential source (evidence for the auth plan).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> users are served by the cluster, but we have only <em>assumed</em> it survives a node loss. Untested failover has a poor record. Phase E turns the assumption into evidence.</p>

    <h3>Phase E - Failover proof</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> <code>kubectl drain</code> of .126 leaves the site fully serving from .199, demonstrated and recorded. This is the evidence that the manual cutover is dead.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Drain .126 &rarr; confirm ingress routes only to the .199 pod; app stays up.</li>
        <li>Repeat in the opposite direction (drain .199).</li>
        <li>Pull the power/network on a node for an unplanned-failure test, not just a graceful drain.</li>
        <li>Record measured recovery time; this is the first real input to the 99% budget.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project E todos:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>E.0 ROLE EVAL:</strong> re-measure each node under realistic load &mdash; does the surviving node have capacity to carry 100% of traffic alone? A 2-core .126 absorbing all load is a real question, not a formality.</li>
        <li>E.1 Graceful drain of node 1 &rarr; site stays up.</li>
        <li>E.2 Graceful drain of node 2 &rarr; site stays up.</li>
        <li>E.3 <strong>Unplanned failure test</strong> (pull power/network), not just a drain.</li>
        <li>E.4 Test under <em>load</em>, including while the AI workload is saturating a node.</li>
        <li>E.5 Record measured recovery time &mdash; first real input to the 99% budget.</li>
        <li>E.6 Re-evaluate rung 2 (3-node) using this evidence (&sect;3f).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> the application layer is now genuinely HA and proven. The remaining hard SPOF is the database: losing .20 takes down both nodes at once, so the 99% target is still arithmetically out of reach (&sect;7). Phase F is not optional if 99% is the goal.</p>

    <h3>Phase F - DB HA (.198 promoted to live replica)</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> .198 assessed against .20 for drift, and either promoted to a live replica (DB SPOF closed) or explicitly confirmed backup-only  -  decision recorded here.</p>
    <p>Until this phase lands, <strong>the site cannot reach 99%</strong>: .20 is a single point of failure whose loss takes down both app nodes at once. App HA without DB HA caps real availability at the availability of one database server.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>F.0 Assess (probe, do not assume):</strong> .198 hardware headroom, Docker status, MariaDB/MySQL version parity with .20, and actual schema/row drift. Its SSH credentials differ from .126 and must be supplied by the user.</li>
        <li><strong>F.1 Decide:</strong> MariaDB async replication (simpler, near-zero risk to .20) vs Galera synchronous cluster (stronger, heavier). <em>Recommendation: async replica first</em>  -  it closes most of the risk without endangering the working primary.</li>
        <li><strong>F.2 Implement</strong> replication .20 &rarr; .198 with monitored lag.</li>
        <li><strong>F.3 Failover procedure:</strong> documented and rehearsed promotion of .198. The app already supports prioritised DB failover via <code>db_config.json</code> priorities  -  wire .198 as priority 2 rather than inventing a new mechanism.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project F todos:</strong> F.0 assess (currently BLOCKED &mdash; see F.1), F.1 dependency AUTH-R4 (.198 SSH credentials are in no managed store; raise on auth plan), F.2 decide async vs Galera, F.3 implement replication with lag monitoring, F.4 wire .198 as priority 2 in the existing failover mechanism, F.5 rehearse promotion, F.6 role eval: is .198 actually capable, or is another host the better replica?</p>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> availability is now genuinely defensible. What remains is <em>effort</em>: every release is still a human running a rollout command at the right moment. Phase G removes that, and is where the "proven container reaches all nodes without touching each one" requirement is fully paid off.</p>

    <h3>Phase G - Automated propagation of a proven image</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> a build promoted on .199 reaches both production pods with <strong>no human action on any node</strong>, and a deliberately bad build is refused by the gate rather than reaching users.</p>
    <p>Phases A&ndash;F make rollout reliable and recoverable; they do not make it automatic. This phase closes &sect;3a.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Promotion by digest:</strong> CI builds once on .199, runs the test/health gate, and promotes that exact digest. <code>:latest</code> is retired  -  a floating tag makes "proven" unprovable.</li>
        <li><strong>Pull-based or push-based, decided on evidence:</strong> either a GitOps agent on-cluster reconciling a manifest repo (pull), or the existing deploy pipeline running <code>kubectl set image</code> (push). <em>Recommendation: start push-based</em>, since it reuses the deploy tooling that already exists and is far less to learn; move to GitOps only if drift becomes a real problem.</li>
        <li><strong>Rolling update with <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code>:</strong> a new pod must pass readiness before an old one is removed  -  a bad build stalls the rollout instead of taking the site down.</li>
        <li><strong>Automatic rollback</strong> on failed rollout; <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> as the rehearsed manual path.</li>
        <li>Staging environment on the cluster so "proven" means proven <em>somewhere real</em> before production.</li>
        <li>This phase depends on the credentials decision (&sect;11): automated rollout must not require a human to hand-place a secret file on a node.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project G todos:</strong> G.0 role eval (which host builds images? .199 is the GPU/AI box and is saturated &mdash; building there may be slow or unavailable exactly when needed), G.1 dependency AUTH-R1/R3 (automated rollout cannot require hand-placed secrets), G.2 build-once pipeline with digest promotion, G.3 retire <code>:latest</code>, G.4 rolling update with <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code>, G.5 automatic rollback on failed rollout, G.6 staging environment, G.7 prove a bad build is refused before reaching users.</p>
    <p><strong>WHY NEXT:</strong> at this point availability, balance and propagation are all solved on k3s. Phase H is therefore <strong>conditional, not scheduled</strong>  -  see its trigger.</p>

    <h3>Phase H - Full Kubernetes (conditional; do not schedule)</h3>
    <p><strong>GATE:</strong> only begin if a documented trigger below is actually observed. Absent a trigger, the correct decision is to <em>stay on k3s</em> and record that as the end state.</p>
    <p>Because k3s is conformant Kubernetes, the manifests written in Phase C already are the Kubernetes end state; this phase changes the control plane, not the application. Realistic triggers:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Control-plane HA needed:</strong> today .126 is the single k3s server. If losing it during a failure becomes unacceptable, move to 3 control-plane nodes with embedded etcd  -  note this is available <em>in k3s</em>, so it is a Phase H.1 step, not a distribution change.</li>
        <li><strong>Scale beyond a handful of nodes</strong>, or a need for features/operators k3s does not carry.</li>
        <li><strong>Organisational requirement</strong> (compliance, managed cloud, external ops support).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Sub-project H todos:</strong> H.0 role eval for any new/external node, H.1 3-node embedded-etcd control plane (rung 2 &mdash; this is a k3s feature, not a distribution change), H.2 evaluate a ZeroTier offsite node as a worker (rung 3; poor etcd peer due to WAN latency, good worker), H.3 evaluate external/cloud quorum member (rung 4), H.4 decide and record whether to climb or stop.</p>
    <p>Honest assessment for the current estate: <strong>H.1 (control-plane HA) is likely worth doing eventually; a full distribution swap is likely never needed.</strong> Recording that prevents this plan from being re-fragmented later by a doc that assumes a big-bang Kubernetes migration.</p>

    <h2 id="shared-state">6. Shared state  -  the thing that breaks naive replica scaling</h2>
    <p>Running two replicas is trivial; running two replicas <em>correctly</em> is not. Each item below is an outage or a support ticket if missed:</p>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">State</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Risk with 2 replicas</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Resolution</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Sessions (<code>comserv2_sessions</code>)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Node-local &rarr; users randomly logged out as they bounce between pods</td><td style="padding:6px;">Shared NFS volume or redis-backed sessions</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Uploads (<code>comserv2_nfs_data</code>)</td><td style="padding:6px;">File uploaded to pod A is invisible on pod B</td><td style="padding:6px;">Existing NFS mount into both pods</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Logs</td><td style="padding:6px;">Split across nodes; the 2026-08-07 diagnosis was crippled by lost container logs</td><td style="padding:6px;">Ship to the DB/system_log (already the audited path) rather than pod-local files</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Cron / scheduled jobs</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>Double execution</strong>  -  two pods each firing the same job</td><td style="padding:6px;">k8s CronJob (single owner), never in-pod cron</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Whisper / heavy tool volumes</td><td style="padding:6px;">Duplicated large caches on both nodes</td><td style="padding:6px;">Portable network tool-services, per existing standard</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h2 id="maths">7. Availability maths  -  what actually buys the 99%</h2>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Stage</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Surviving faults</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Still fatal</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">Today</td><td style="padding:6px;">none  -  container, host, or DB loss = outage</td><td style="padding:6px;">everything</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">After A-E</td><td style="padding:6px;">app crash, bad deploy, one app node down</td><td style="padding:6px;">DB .20, registry (deploys only), OPNsense</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">After F</td><td style="padding:6px;">+ database host loss</td><td style="padding:6px;">OPNsense / WAN entry, site-wide power</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Honest conclusion:</strong> Phases A-E remove the <em>most frequent</em> causes of downtime (which is what actually bit us on 2026-08-07), but <strong>Phase F is required for a defensible 99% claim</strong>. The remaining single points after F  -  the OPNsense entry point and site power  -  should be acknowledged to clients rather than papered over.</p>

    <h2 id="spofs">8. Explicitly retained SPOFs (accepted, with rationale)</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Registry on .199</strong>  -  if .199 is lost, running pods keep serving; only new deploys stop. Acceptable.</li>
        <li><strong>k3s control plane on .126</strong>  -  single server node. Workloads keep running if the control plane blips; a full HA control plane needs a third node and is out of scope.</li>
        <li><strong>.199 is also the developer workstation</strong>  -  a genuine tension: production capacity shares a machine that gets rebooted for development. Revisit if it proves disruptive; a dedicated third node is the fix.</li>
        <li><strong>OPNsense / WAN entry</strong>  -  unchanged by this plan.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="gates">9. Gates / Definition of Done</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>[ ] Registry on .199 pushes from .199 and pulls on .126.</li>
        <li>[ ] <code>kubectl get nodes</code> &rarr; 2 Ready.</li>
        <li>[ ] Deployment replicas=2, both pods Running, sharing sessions/NFS/redis.</li>
        <li>[ ] Probes tuned to the real ~90s startup; no restart kill-loop under load.</li>
        <li>[ ] External entry hits the k3s ingress; app serves identically to before.</li>
        <li>[ ] Drain .126 &rarr; site stays up on .199 (manual cutover eliminated).</li>
        <li>[ ] Unplanned-failure test (hard node kill) passes, recovery time recorded.</li>
        <li>[ ] No DB credential in image layers (IMGSEC verified).</li>
        <li>[ ] .198 drift assessed; replica-vs-backup decision recorded.</li>
        <li>[ ] Scheduled jobs run exactly once cluster-wide.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="consolidation">10. Consolidation record  -  what was absorbed, what was retired</h2>
    <p>Nothing was deleted. Superseded documents are retained for history; their durable content is listed here so it is not lost.</p>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Document</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Disposition</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">What was kept</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>K3S_APP_HA_PLAN.tt</code> (this file)</td><td style="padding:6px;"><strong>PROMOTED to master</strong></td><td style="padding:6px;">Whole 2-node bare-metal design; still the spine of &sect;3-&sect;5</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><a href="/Documentation/K3S_PRODUCTION_MIGRATION_GUIDE" style="color: var(--link-color);">K3S_PRODUCTION_MIGRATION_GUIDE</a></td><td style="padding:6px;">SUPERSEDED (Dec 2025, Proxmox/Galera on .198)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Galera + replication mechanics and rollback steps &rarr; retargeted into Phase F</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;"><a href="/Documentation/PRODUCTION_DB_KUBERNETES_MIGRATION_PLAN" style="color: var(--link-color);">PRODUCTION_DB_KUBERNETES_MIGRATION_PLAN</a></td><td style="padding:6px;">SUPERSEDED (Dec 2025, 3-host Proxmox)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Zero-downtime cutover discipline and rollback procedure &rarr; Phase D</td></tr>
