    <p style="color: var(--text-muted-color); font-size: var(--font-size-small);"><strong>Correction of record:</strong> every superseded document above refers to <strong>192.168.1.198</strong> as the production database and assumes a 3-host Proxmox cluster. Verified 2026-08-07: the primary database is <strong>192.168.1.20:3307</strong> (MariaDB <code>ency</code>, <code>db-server-1</code>, priority 1) and production is bare metal. Treat any surviving statement to the contrary in those files as obsolete.</p>

    <!-- SEE ALSO SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">

    <h2 id="credentials">11. Authentication &amp; credentials &mdash; a DEPENDENCY, not part of this plan</h2>
    <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--warning-color); padding: var(--spacing-small); margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <strong>Scope correction.</strong> An earlier draft of this plan tried to <em>decide</em> the credentials strategy. That was wrong and is retracted. The credential design flaw &mdash; the original assumption that <strong>each table needed its own credential and that these belonged in JSON files</strong> &mdash; was created at the very beginning of the process and is a <strong>separate problem with its own plan</strong>. This plan does not own it, must not redesign it, and must not quietly work around it.
    </div>
    <p><strong>The rule:</strong> where HA/load-balancing work <em>requires</em> a change in the authentication/credential system, this plan <strong>states the requirement and raises a todo on the authentication plan</strong> &mdash; it does not implement the change here. That todo may <strong>block</strong> progression of this plan, and that is the correct and accepted outcome. A blocked phase is better than two plans silently redesigning the same subsystem in different directions.</p>
    <p>Owning plan: <a href="/Documentation/AuthenticationEvolutionPlan" style="color: var(--link-color);">Authentication Evolution Plan</a> (marked ACTIVE &mdash; DO NOT CHANGE WITHOUT EXPLICIT PERMISSION, which is a further reason this plan must not edit it).</p>

    <h3>11a. What this plan REQUIRES from the authentication plan</h3>
    <p>These are requirements raised <em>on</em> the auth plan, each to become a todo <strong>in that plan's project</strong>, blocking the phase named:</p>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--spacing-medium) 0;">
        <tr style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Req</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">What HA needs</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Why the current design blocks it</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Blocks</th></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">AUTH-R1</td><td style="padding:6px;">Credentials deliverable to a pod without a human placing a file on each node</td><td style="padding:6px;">Per-file JSON on hosts cannot propagate automatically; defeats the whole point of &sect;3a</td><td style="padding:6px;">Phase C / G</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">AUTH-R2</td><td style="padding:6px;">Sessions valid across BOTH replicas</td><td style="padding:6px;">Node-local sessions log users out at random when balanced (&sect;6)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Phase D</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">AUTH-R3</td><td style="padding:6px;">One credential set per <em>connection</em>, not per table</td><td style="padding:6px;">The original per-table assumption multiplies secrets to rotate per node</td><td style="padding:6px;">Phase G</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:6px;">AUTH-R4</td><td style="padding:6px;">Managed credentials for .198 and any new node</td><td style="padding:6px;">.198 SSH creds are in no managed store &mdash; this already blocked Phase F.0</td><td style="padding:6px;">Phase F</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><strong>Observation only, decision NOT taken here:</strong> <code>RemoteDB.pm::_load_config</code> already reads mounted secret files first, then <code>COMSERV_DB_*</code> env, then <code>db_config.json</code> (which the code itself logs as DEPRECATED). This is recorded as <em>evidence for the auth plan to use</em>. Whether that ladder is the right end state is the authentication plan's call, not this plan's.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--spacing-small);"><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">&uarr; Back to Top</a></p>

